<Page title="How We Test and Research" description="The criteria GarageRecoveryLab uses to evaluate saunas, cold plunges, hot tubs, massage chairs and garage gym gear.">
  <p>Every guide states which products we used ourselves, which we inspected in a showroom, and which we evaluated from specifications and verified owner reports. We never present research as hands-on testing.</p>

  <h2>What we measure</h2>
  <h3>Saunas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Heat-up time and peak temperature, versus the manufacturer's claim</li>
    <li>Electrical requirements (120V plug-in or 240V hardwired) and the real install cost</li>
    <li>Running cost per session at the U.S. average electricity rate</li>
    <li>EMF readings where the brand makes low-EMF claims</li>
    <li>Wood, build quality, and warranty terms, including what voids them</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Cold plunges</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Lowest stable temperature and time to cool from tap water</li>
    <li>Filtration, sanitation and how often water needs changing</li>
    <li>Chiller noise, power draw and indoor versus outdoor suitability</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Hot tubs and swim spas</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Purchase price, delivery and site prep, and monthly energy cost</li>
    <li>Insulation, cover quality, jets and seating layout</li>
    <li>Dealer network and warranty service in practice</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Massage chairs and garage gym gear</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Fit across body sizes, track length, and noise</li>
    <li>Footprint, build quality and warranty</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Prices and updates</h2>
  <p>Prices are checked against the seller's site and dated in every comparison table. We review each guide at least every six months and when a product changes.</p>

  <h2>Health claims</h2>
  <p>Heat and cold exposure are not risk-free. We cite published research for any health claim and never promise outcomes. Talk to your doctor before starting, especially if you are pregnant or have a heart condition.</p>
</Page>
